# Cargo test terminal discipline

When verifying one Rust test from the agent terminal, pick the **narrowest** target so Cargo does not compile every integration test crate.

| Test lives in | Command |
| --- | --- |
| `src/` unit test (`#[test] fn foo`) | `cargo test foo --lib` |
| Integration file `tests/bar.rs` (whole crate) | `cargo test --test bar` |
| One fn inside that integration file | `cargo test --test bar foo` |

- **Never** bare `cargo test <fn_name>` without `--lib` or `--test <stem>` — Cargo may build/link **all** `tests/*.rs` binaries before running a single lib test (10–25+ minutes on this repo).
- Integration file stems are **not** fn-name filters; use `--test <stem>` (see `30-benchmarks-tests.mdc`).

## Output and concurrency

- Do **not** pipe `cargo test` through `tail`, `head`, or similar unless you accept **no visible output** until the process exits (looks hung during compiles).
- Do not launch multiple `cargo test` / `cargo build` commands in parallel in one turn — they block on Cargo's build-directory lock.

## Verify the run

Confirm output includes one of:

- `Running unittests src/lib.rs` (lib unit test), or
- `Running tests/<stem>.rs` (integration test),

and `N passed` with **N > 0** — not `0 passed; … filtered out` across many binaries.

## Timeouts

- After code changes, first `--lib` compile: allow ~2–3 minutes (`block_until_ms` ≥ 120000).
- Re-runs with warm artifacts: ~30–60s is usually enough.
